      "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{6}{5{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.$",
      "worked_solution": "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{5{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{6}{5}\\int {y}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{(-4+1)}}{-4+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{-3}}{-3})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{15}\\cdot {y}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5{y}^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
